# Web app

Server Components by default. `"use client"` only where interactivity requires
it. Import across folders with the `@/` alias, never `../..`.

## Forms use TanStack Form and the `field` primitive

```tsx
const form = useForm({
  defaultValues: { email: "", password: "" },
  validators: {
    onSubmit: schema,
    onSubmitAsync: ({ value }) => submitToServer(() => authClient.signIn.email(value)),
  },
  onSubmit: () => router.push("/dashboard"),
});
```

The server call goes in `onSubmitAsync`, not in `onSubmit`. Better Auth reports
failure by returning `{ error }` rather than throwing, so a validator is what
turns that into a message and stops the success handler from running.
`submitToServer` does that conversion, including the network-failure case.

Its result must carry `fields`, even empty — TanStack Form only treats a result
as form-level when that key is present, and `{ form }` alone reaches the UI as
an object instead of the message. Read the message back with `serverError`,
which drops the Zod record that shares the slot and is already rendered per
field.

Every `<form>` carries `noValidate`. `type="email"` otherwise lets the browser
cancel submission before any handler runs, so the schema never gets a say and
the page shows nothing — a button that silently does nothing.
`e2e/validation.spec.ts` is what holds that in place.

Markup comes from `@repo/ui/components/field`: `Field`, `FieldLabel`,
`FieldError`. `FieldError` renders `role="alert"`, which the e2e specs assert
on. It is form-library agnostic, so it stays put if the state layer changes.

Read `isSubmitting` through `form.Subscribe`. Field state is subscribed per
field, so reading `form.state` in the body will not re-render.

## A form with no fields is still a form

Accepting an invitation and resending a verification email have no inputs but do
have a pending state and a server error. They use `useForm` with
`defaultValues: {}` rather than a bespoke hook, so there is one submission
pattern in the app.

## A client-side permission check is cosmetic

```tsx
const canDelete = role?.authorize({ post: ["delete"] }).success ?? false;
```

That decides whether a control renders. The server checks the same permission on
every call, and the mutation is scoped by organization as well. Never treat the
client check as the boundary.

## After a navigation that depends on the session, refresh

`router.push()` alone leaves the router cache holding a page rendered for the
previous session. Follow it with `router.refresh()` whenever the destination is
a Server Component that reads the session.

## Redirect targets from the URL must be narrowed

`?next=` is attacker-controlled. Pass it through `safeRedirect` — `//evil.example`
is a protocol-relative URL a browser will follow off-site.
